Z||000038||malacite||11-23-2001||05:47 PM||||or you could have been like me.<BR>i spent $2000 total on fixing up that car plus the $750 for buying it, minus the $250 i got for selling it.<BR>that's $2500!<BR>then it blew up, limped to the side of the road (actually i pushed it) and i never got to drive it again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>the guy who bought the car (for a 3800 donor project) let me keep the wheels and tires to make back some of my money.<BR>but that deal fell through three times!<BR>now i got tires i can't even use!!!!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">arrrrrgggggg! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>sorry,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> i got carried away.<BR>hey, if your wheels ever got bad, i know a guy who's got a set...cheap.<P>------------------<BR>Malacite<P>Grendel-88 Fiero RIP<BR>'94 Saturn SW2 RallySport<BR>'95 Saturn SL1 Touring||||reg||9
Z||000039||thomas_l||11-24-2001||02:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by avengador1:<BR><B>Remember Caveat Emptor, let the buyer beware.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>AMEN. Welcome to the world of trading used cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I'm sorry you had problems with your sale and don't mean to sound like an ass but you made 2 classic mistakes (as I have done in the past too - dont feel like the Lone Ranger):<P>1. Project/hobby cars make poor choices for a daily driver.<BR>2. Trading horses is not a good thing to do when you are Living On A Shoestring Budget (based on the missed job and family with one working car thing).<P>Bottom line is you bought a 16 year old car. At that point you are pretty much on your own with respect to knowing what you are getting into. After about 10 years, the odometer is meaningless except when you're buying a "rare" car - in which case that odo reading will be supported with other docs like service records. If you're not a mechanic, bring one along or pay one for a used car inspection. At that point, you still are driving off in an old car that is more prone to break than a new one. It's nobodys fault - just an old car. You win some you lose some. It sucks. You are minus $2150 and plus a Fiero with a few problems. Maybe the next one will be a perfect car that lasts for years with just oil & gas. You never know. I have 10 yrs automotive service background, well equipped garage, and still get stung once in a while. I can usually spot signs of trouble and fix broken things but I still don't have a crystal ball to forecast the future with. My rule of thumb is always plan on anything costing 2X what you think it's going to and stay outta the grocery money. Good luck & I hope someone on Pennocks in your neck of the US will volunteer some time to help you get your 85 roadworthy. I'd go but IL is too damn far and I already blew my vacation and spare money on.... A PROJECT CAR!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000040||Mach10||11-24-2001||02:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by thomas_l:<BR><B> AMEN. Welcome to the world of trading used cars   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I'm sorry you had problems with your sale and don't mean to sound like an ass but you made 2 classic mistakes (as I have done in the past too - dont feel like the Lone Ranger):<P>1. Project/hobby cars make poor choices for a daily driver.<BR>2. Trading horses is not a good thing to do when you are Living On A Shoestring Budget (based on the missed job and family with one working car thing).<P>Bottom line is you bought a 16 year old car. At that point you are pretty much on your own with respect to knowing what you are getting into. After about 10 years, the odometer is meaningless except when you're buying a "rare" car - in which case that odo reading will be supported with other docs like service records. If you're not a mechanic, bring one along or pay one for a used car inspection. At that point, you still are driving off in an old car that is more prone to break than a new one. It's nobodys fault - just an old car. You win some you lose some. It sucks. You are minus $2150 and plus a Fiero with a few problems. Maybe the next one will be a perfect car that lasts for years with just oil & gas. You never know. I have 10 yrs automotive service background, well equipped garage, and still get stung once in a while. I can usually spot signs of trouble and fix broken things but I still don't have a crystal ball to forecast the future with. My rule of thumb is always plan on anything costing 2X what you think it's going to and stay outta the grocery money. Good luck & I hope someone on Pennocks in your neck of the US will volunteer some time to help you get your 85 roadworthy. I'd go but IL is too damn far and I already blew my vacation and spare money on.... A PROJECT CAR!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hehehe... Not necessarily... My Fiero has been very good to me. Less break-downs (read: none) than may parents brand new minivan... It WAS on warranty (for the first while) so it didn' cost none... Nah, it waited till 10k AFTER the warranty to break all the BIG stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000041||SLYGUY||11-24-2001||10:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>I repeat, unless it's slipping (I only read the top of your post), it is the hydraulics/linkage (needs something replaced, not a bleeding), not the actual <BR>clutch.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree. <P>The clutch on my car deteriorated until it wouldn't disengage at all. Replaced the master cylinder with a NEW one from GM and it was much better. I still have a lot of air in the system and am bleeding it out bit by bit. <P>If it was a mechanical problem, bleeding it would not temporarily resolve the situation. Bleeding may help, but if it is mechanical it will be quite obvious. <P>Triad...you have AIR<P>Lots and lots of AIR...<P>Sly<P>||||reg||1
Z||000042||MrPBody||11-24-2001||11:51 PM||||In 1970, I bought my first car -- a 1964 Jaguar sedan -- from the <I>basement</I> of a declining dealership in downtown Atlanta (back in the days when there were auto dealerships downtown.)<P>A few days later, I gave a guy whose Alfa died a ride to Macon (about 80 miles); on the way back, it developed a rod knock.  A problem with the car?  No, it must have been something I did, but the dealer split the repair cost with me.  A few months later I drove it to Sebring, burning a valve.  I think the dealer repaired that too, but at my expense.<P>After correcting a few of the dealership mechanic's "oversights," I figured-out that I could do better work than they.  In the end, I learned enough to repair cars for a living -- and did so for a dozen years.<P>Then a couple of years later, I learned -- the hard way -- that the Jag's lovely dark green paint concealed a half-inch of Bondo on the right front fender.<P>By the time that I understood that I had been hosed, any hope of recourse had gone, and anyhow the dealership was out of business.<P>On the good side, it was a great -- and great-looking -- car to drive, and I learned lots about cars . . . and car dealers.<P>So . . .<P>Sorry you're going through this.  The experience is painful but I hope not fatal.<P>I agree that the clutch problem probably is due to pedal or hydraulics, not the clutch itself.<P>The headliner may cost less than you think.  If the backing board is reusable, you can fix it yourself for about $50 in materials.<P>A Haynes manual is better than nothing.  A GM/Helm manual is better but five times as expensive.<P>After you drive the 4-speed for a while, you may decide that you don't need a 5-speed.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000043||TRiAD||11-25-2001||05:14 PM||||Wow...<P><BR>Just an update, the clutch pedal is here, I've been trying to R&R the pedal, and now need a bigger socket set (for that nut holding the common brake-clutch shaft in). <P>It's going to be a bit before I get anywhere. <P>Diagnosing and bleeding the hydraulics is next...<P>Thanks again!<BR>||||reg||1
